#: ../pwntools/docs/source/intro.rst:31
msgid "Making Connections"
msgstr ""

#: ../pwntools/docs/source/intro.rst:33
msgid ""
"You need to talk to the challenge binary in order to pwn it, right? "
"pwntools makes this stupid simple with its :mod:`pwnlib.tubes` module."
msgstr ""

#: ../pwntools/docs/source/intro.rst:36
msgid ""
"This exposes a standard interface to talk to processes, sockets, serial "
"ports, and all manner of things, along with some nifty helpers for common"
" tasks. For example, remote connections via :mod:`pwnlib.tubes.remote`."
msgstr ""

#: ../pwntools/docs/source/intro.rst:52
msgid "It's also easy to spin up a listener"
msgstr ""

#: ../pwntools/docs/source/intro.rst:61
msgid "Interacting with processes is easy thanks to :mod:`pwnlib.tubes.process`."
msgstr ""

#: ../pwntools/docs/source/intro.rst:73
msgid ""
"Not only can you interact with processes programmatically, but you can "
"actually **interact** with processes."
msgstr ""

#: ../pwntools/docs/source/intro.rst:80
msgid ""
"There's even an SSH module for when you've got to SSH into a box to "
"perform a local/setuid exploit with :mod:`pwnlib.tubes.ssh`.  You can "
"quickly spawn processes and grab the output, or spawn a process and "
"interact with it like a ``process`` tube."
msgstr ""

#: ../pwntools/docs/source/intro.rst:100
msgid "Packing Integers"
msgstr ""

#: ../pwntools/docs/source/intro.rst:102
msgid ""
"A common task for exploit-writing is converting between integers as "
"Python sees them, and their representation as a sequence of bytes. "
"Usually folks resort to the built-in ``struct`` module."
msgstr ""

#: ../pwntools/docs/source/intro.rst:106
msgid ""
"pwntools makes this easier with :mod:`pwnlib.util.packing`.  No more "
"remembering unpacking codes, and littering your code with helper "
"routines."
msgstr ""

#: ../pwntools/docs/source/intro.rst:116
msgid "The packing/unpacking operations are defined for many common bit-widths."
msgstr ""

